    "Cardiac catheterization is recommended at the time of transcatheter ASD closure to evaluate pulmonary artery pressures and shunt magnitude. A diagnostic catheterization may be needed for hemodynamic details. Patients with reduced capacity and moderate or large left-to-right shunts benefit from closure. Not addressing ASD leads to worse outcomes, including arrhythmias and greater PAH. Surgical evaluation should consider diastolic dysfunction. Cyanosis is not a contraindication to closure, though certain cases of TR with shunting may occur. Long-term outcomes after closure tend to improve. Percutaneous ASD closure devices for secundum-type defects are approved, but other ASDs require surgery. Patients with significant symptoms and a secundum ASD should receive closure at surgery time. PAH and ASD evaluation requires assessing shunt impact despite elevated pressures. Optimal management includes medication and collaboration with ACHD and pulmonary hypertension experts. Favorable prognosis is associated with PAH therapy success. Risks exist if surgery is attempted with open shunts and Eisenmenger syndrome.": "genes",
